  "verbalization": "Nifedipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is linked to Cacna1c through a shared lineage in the hetionet dataset. The evidence suggests that Nifedipine, as part of the dihydropyridine class, binds to Cacna1c via its inclusion in the broader category of calcium channel antagonists. This connection is reinforced by the fact that both Nifedipine and Clevidipine, which are included in the dihydropyridine family, directly interact with Cacna1c. The lowest common ancestors of these entities—such as the dihydropyridine class—highlight a common mechanism of action, where the drug class itself is a key intermediary in the relationship between Nifedipine and Cacna1c. The integration of multiple paths supports a unified explanation, emphasizing the role of calcium channel blockade in the pharmacological interaction between Nifedipine and Cacna1c.",
